must be retained)\n    - else:\n        - Check if any child is in retained set (i.e., child id not in removed_ids). If yes, skip (cannot remove because retained children).\n        - Check if after removal, all required symbols still present. That is: new_freq = copy? Actually we need to check if node['symbol'] is required and its count becomes zero after removal. Since we have not yet removed it, we can check: current freq for that symbol is at least 1. If it is exactly 1 and that node is the only provider, then removing would reduce count to 0 -> cannot remove. But if there is another node with same symbol that is retained (i.e., not removed), then count would still be >=1 after removal. So condition: if node['symbol'] in required_symbols and freq[node['symbol']] == 1 and there is no other retained node with that symbol? Since we are removing this node, the count would become zero. However, we must also consider that there might be other nodes with same symbol that are not yet removed but might be removed later? But since we process deeper first, any node with same symbol at shallower depth will be processed later. If we remove this node now, and later we remove the other node that also provides that symbol, then that required symbol would disappear. But the condition \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" is about the state after removal, considering all nodes that are currently retained and will be retained? The requirement is that after the entire reduction, all required symbols must be present. So we need to ensure that we do not remove the last occurrence of a required symbol. So we need to consider future removals? We cannot, because we don't know yet which shallower nodes will be removed. The problem says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" It might mean at the moment of removal, after removing this node, all required symbols are still present in the tree (including all nodes