en processed yet, but they might also provide that symbol? No, if it's the only provider, removing would cause missing). If there is another provider at shallower depth, and we remove the deeper one, later the shallower one might be considered for removal. But if the shallower one is also the last remaining provider, then we would not remove it because removal would cause missing. So the greedy approach works if we always check after removal whether all required symbols are still present in the current set of retained nodes (including nodes not yet considered). That means we can only remove a node if there is at least one other retained node with the same symbol that will not be removed? But at the time of considering the deeper node, the shallower node is still retained (since not processed yet). So if the deeper node is the only provider among nodes processed so far? Actually we need to consider all nodes in the tree, not just processed. Since we check after removal (hypothetically removing this node), we need to check if the remaining nodes (all nodes except removed_ids and this node) still cover all required symbols. That includes shallower nodes that we haven't decided yet. So it's fine: if a required symbol appears in a shallower node that is non-removable, then removing the deeper node is okay. If the shallower node is removable, we might later remove it, but at that later time we will again check if required symbols are still covered, and that will prevent removing the last one. So the greedy process is safe: we remove nodes as long as after removal, the current set of nodes (including all nodes not yet removed) satisfies required symbols. This ensures that at the end, the final set satisfies required symbols. Because any removal that would cause violation at the end would also cause violation at the moment of removal (since later removals only remove more nodes, not add). So if we check after each removal that the current set still covers required symbols, the final set will also cover them. So the algorithm is correct.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Parse nodes list.\n2. Build dict node_by_id.\n3. For each node, compute children list: iterate nodes, add child to parent's children list.\n4. Initialize counts for required symbols: count_sym = Counter(symbol for all nodes). But we only care about required symbols.\n5. Determine processing order: sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending (string compare). Because ID is string, but we can sort as strings.\n6. For each node in that order:\n   - if not node['removable']: continue (will be retained)\n   - Check if node has any child that is not in removed_ids. If yes, skip (cannot remove because child retained).\n   - Check if removing this node would cause any required symbol to disappear: new_counts = count_sym.copy(); new_counts[node['symbol']] -= 1; if any required symbol has new_counts <= 0, then skip (cannot remove).\n   - Else: remove it: add node['id'] to removed_ids, update count_sym = new_counts.\n7. After loop, retained_ids = [node['id'] for node in nodes if node['id'] not in removed_ids]\n8. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": list(removed_ids in order of removal? We need to return removal order. We have processed in order, and we added to removed_ids as we go. So removed_ids is a list? We can use a list to preserve order. So we will maintain a list removed_order. Then final removed_node_ids = removed_order. retained_node_ids = sorted? The required says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted? It says \"sorted retained IDs\". We need to sort retained node IDs. Probably lexicographically. We'll sort them.\n9. Return JSON.\n\nEdge cases: \n- root is non-removable, but may have children removable.\n- required_symbols include symbols that may appear multiple times.\n- The tree may have nodes with same symbol.\n\nWe need to be careful: the condition \"Never remove a node with retained children\" means we cannot remove a node if any of its children is still retained (i.e., not removed). Since we process children before parent, if a child is removable but we decided not to remove it (maybe because it fails required symbol check), then parent cannot be removed. So that's fine.\n\nAlso note: the required_symbols list includes \"root\" symbol. Root's symbol is \"root\". So we must ensure that symbol \"root\" is always present. Since root is not removable, that's fine.\n\nNow, we must implement solve(case) that takes the payload and returns the dict. Ensure no mutation of case.\n\nWe'll write Python code.\n\nTesting with provided fixtures: the three cases have same structure. Let's manually reason for first case:\nnodes: root (depth0, not removable, symbol root), n1 (depth1, not removable, s1), n2 (depth1, removable, s2), n3 (depth1, not removable, s0), n4 (depth2, removable, s1, parent n2), n5 (depth2, not removable, s2, parent n3), n6 (depth2, removable, s0, parent n1).\nrequired_symbols: root, s1.\nProcess depth descending: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. Order by ID: n4, n5, n6 (assuming lex order n4 < n5 < n6). n4: removable, check children: none? n4 has no children (since ids n? only up to n6). So no retained children issue. Check required symbols: symbol s1. Current count: s1 appears in n1 and n4. Removing n4 would leave count of s1 = 1 (n1). So allowed. So remove n4. Update counts. n5: not removable, skip. n6: removable, no children. symbol s0, not required, so removal doesn't affect required symbols. Check required: still root and s1 (s1 still from n1). So can remove n6. Remove n6. Then depth1 nodes: n1 (not removable), n2 (removable), n3 (not removable). Order by ID: n1, n2, n3? Actually depth1, IDs: n1, n2, n3. n1 not removable. n2: removable, check children: n4 was removed, so no child retained. Check required: symbol s2, not required, so fine. Remove n2. n3 not removable. Then root not removable. So removed_order = [n4, n6, n2]. retained_ids = all others: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted: maybe n1, n3, n5, root. But we need to sort IDs as strings: \"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\". That seems correct.\n\nFor second case, similar but utilities differ; the algorithm is deterministic based on structure and removable flags, not on utility. So answer should be same for all three cases? Let's check case 1 and 2: same structure, same removable flags, same required symbols. So removal order and retained set should be identical across these three fixtures? Yes, because the algorithm only depends on tree structure, required symbols, and removable flags. It does not depend on utility values. So all three will produce same output. That's fine.\n\nWe need to implement and return the solve function.\n\nNow, output format: as per instruction, the entire response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\", value being the Python source code as a string. So we need to write the code and then JSON-encode it.\n\nWe'll write the function solve(case) that receives case (the payload dict). We'll parse nodes, compute, and return the result.\n\nWe need to ensure we use only standard library, no external imports except maybe collections.Counter for convenience, but can implement manually. We'll use collections.Counter which is standard. Also we need to sort nodes by depth and id. We'll generate removed_order as list.\n\nI'll write the code as a single string.\n\nPotential pitfalls: need to ensure that we treat IDs as strings for sorting. Use string comparison.\n\nAlso, note that the condition \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" applies even if the node's symbol is not required? Yes, it's about all required symbols, so removal of a node with non-required symbol is generally allowed unless it causes a required symbol to vanish? That can't happen because the node's symbol is not required. So condition is only about required symbols being present. So fine.\n\nWe also have \"Never remove a node with retained children.\" 